You’re a DevOps Engineer… and You Don’t Know These 8 Things?

Then interviews - and production - will be brutal.

Here are 8 non-negotiables 👇

𝟏. 𝐂𝐈/𝐂𝐃 𝐀𝐫𝐜𝐡𝐢𝐭𝐞𝐜𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐞
You should clearly explain:
• How code moves from commit → build → test → artifact → deploy
• Where approvals happen
• How rollbacks are handled
• How secrets are managed
If you can’t draw your pipeline end-to-end, that’s a red flag.

𝟐. 𝐂𝐥𝐨𝐮𝐝 𝐅𝐮𝐧𝐝𝐚𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐚𝐥𝐬
Whether it’s
 • Amazon Web Services
 • Microsoft Azure
 • Google Cloud

You must understand:
• IAM & permissions
• Networking (VPC, subnets, routing)
• Load balancing
• Auto scaling
• Cost implications
Not theory - architecture thinking.

𝟑. 𝐈𝐧𝐟𝐫𝐚𝐬𝐭𝐫𝐮𝐜𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐞 𝐚𝐬 𝐂𝐨𝐝𝐞
If you mention
Terraform

Be ready to answer:
• What is state locking?
• How does remote backend work?
• What if tfstate is corrupted?
• How do you manage multiple environments?
Syntax is easy. State management is real DevOps.

𝟒. 𝐊𝐮𝐛𝐞𝐫𝐧𝐞𝐭𝐞𝐬 𝐓𝐫𝐨𝐮𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬𝐡𝐨𝐨𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠
If you write Kubernetes in resume:

Be ready to debug:
• CrashLoopBackOff
• ImagePullBackOff
• Pending pods
• Service not reachable
• Ingress misconfiguration

Using:
• logs
• describe
• events
• resource limits
Not guessing.

𝟓. 𝐋𝐢𝐧𝐮𝐱 & 𝐍𝐞𝐭𝐰𝐨𝐫𝐤𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐁𝐚𝐬𝐢𝐜𝐬
You should confidently handle:
• Disk full issues
• Memory spikes
• CPU bottlenecks
• Port conflicts
• Permission problems
Many DevOps interviews fail here.

𝟔. 𝐌𝐨𝐧𝐢𝐭𝐨𝐫𝐢𝐧𝐠 & 𝐎𝐛𝐬𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲
You must know:
• What metrics matter (CPU, memory, latency, error rate)
• Alert thresholds
• How to reduce alert noise
• How to perform RCA
Tools don’t matter as much as thinking.

𝟕. 𝐈𝐧𝐜𝐢𝐝𝐞𝐧𝐭 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐔𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐫 𝐏𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐬𝐮𝐫𝐞
Be ready to explain:
“At 2 AM, production went down because…”

Then clearly describe:
• Immediate mitigation
• Root cause
• Permanent fix
• Preventive action
This is where real engineers stand out.

𝟖. 𝐎𝐰𝐧𝐞𝐫𝐬𝐡𝐢𝐩 𝐌𝐢𝐧𝐝𝐬𝐞𝐭
Stop saying:
“We deployed…”
“Our team configured…”

Say:
“I designed…”
“I automated…”
“I troubleshot…”
Ownership builds credibility instantly.